anv: keep HOST_CACHED on exported bos (Xe2 / venus texture corruption)

On Xe2 an allocation from a memory type that advertises HOST_CACHED silently
becomes uncached as soon as it is exportable, and every buffer venus hands to
an ARCVM guest is exportable. Two places conspire:

  * anv_AllocateMemory() takes an early branch for EXTERNAL|SCANOUT that sets
    only ANV_BO_ALLOC_HOST_COHERENT and never looks at the memory type's
    VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT, so the flag is lost.

  * anv_device_get_pat_entry() then matches EXTERNAL|SCANOUT before the
    integrated-platform host-caching rules and returns pat.scanout, which on
    Xe2 is PAT_ENTRY(6, WC) -- CPU write-combining, GPU PAT 6 = XD, not
    snooped. (BSpec 71582. On earlier integrated parts the scanout entry was
    still coherent, which is why taking it unconditionally was harmless.)

The exporter therefore uses the memory uncached while the importer, told
HOST_CACHED by the very same memory type, maps it write-back. Neither side
observes the other's writes. Under ARCVM this shows up as Android apps drawing
uninitialised memory: glyph atlases rendered as white noise, stray geometry
rasterised as thin diagonal streaks across the scene.

Reproduced and fixed with a QEMU venus guest on 8086:64a0 (see
internal/archive/arcvm-venus-hang/). Sweeping every host-visible memory type
with a staging->image->readback / buffer->buffer / GPU-fill round trip:
unpatched, the HOST_CACHED type fails every iteration with ~95% of bytes wrong
in both directions; the non-cached type passes. Patched, both types pass
24/24. lavapipe as the host backend passes either way, which places the fault
in ANV rather than in the venus protocol.

--- a/src/intel/vulkan/anv_device.c
+++ b/src/intel/vulkan/anv_device.c
@@ -1679,6 +1679,16 @@
 
    if (alloc_flags & (ANV_BO_ALLOC_EXTERNAL | ANV_BO_ALLOC_SCANOUT)) {
       alloc_flags |= ANV_BO_ALLOC_HOST_COHERENT;
+      /* Exporting the bo does not release us from the caching the memory type
+       * promised: whoever imports it maps the memory according to those very
+       * flags. Dropping HOST_CACHED here used to be harmless because every
+       * integrated scanout PAT entry was still snooped, but on Xe2 it is not
+       * (pat.scanout is CPU:WC / GPU:XD), so the exporter ends up using the
+       * memory uncached while the importer uses it write-back and the two
+       * never see each other's writes.
+       */
+      if (mem_type->propertyFlags & VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT)
+         alloc_flags |= ANV_BO_ALLOC_HOST_CACHED;
    } else if (mem_type->propertyFlags & VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT) {
       if (mem_type->propertyFlags & VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_COHERENT_BIT)
          alloc_flags |= ANV_BO_ALLOC_HOST_COHERENT;
@@ -2187,6 +2197,25 @@
    if (alloc_flags & ANV_BO_ALLOC_IMPORTED)
       return &device->info->pat.cached_coherent;
 
+   /* An EXTERNAL allocation that the memory type advertised as HOST_CACHED
+    * must keep a cached, coherent PAT entry on integrated platforms.
+    *
+    * pat.scanout is CPU:WC / GPU:XD on Xe2 (BSpec 71582), i.e. genuinely
+    * uncached and not snooped -- on earlier integrated parts the scanout entry
+    * was still coherent, which is why taking it unconditionally used to be
+    * harmless. Handing that bo to an importer which was promised HOST_CACHED
+    * (venus maps it write-back inside a VM guest, per the memory type flags)
+    * leaves the two sides disagreeing about caching: neither observes the
+    * other's writes, and the importer reads uninitialised memory. Real scanout
+    * buffers still need the display-friendly entry, so only EXTERNAL-without-
+    * SCANOUT is redirected.
+    */
+   if ((alloc_flags & (ANV_BO_ALLOC_EXTERNAL | ANV_BO_ALLOC_SCANOUT)) &&
+       !(alloc_flags & ANV_BO_ALLOC_SCANOUT) &&
+       (alloc_flags & ANV_BO_ALLOC_HOST_CACHED) &&
+       !anv_physical_device_has_vram(device->physical))
+      return &device->info->pat.cached_coherent;
+
    if (alloc_flags & (ANV_BO_ALLOC_EXTERNAL | ANV_BO_ALLOC_SCANOUT))
       return &device->info->pat.scanout;
 
